--- /dev/null
+++ b/paper/corrected/paper.tex
@@ -0,0 +1,798 @@
+\documentclass[conference]{IEEEtran}
+
+\usepackage{booktabs}
+\usepackage{graphicx}
+\usepackage{amsmath}
+\usepackage{amssymb}
+\usepackage{microtype}
+\usepackage{xcolor}
+\usepackage{url}
+
+\input{paper/corrected/snippets/corrected_result_macros.tex}
+
+\title{Native MXFP4 Arithmetic for FPGA Gated DeltaNet Decode:\\
+Cost and Synthetic Long-Horizon Recurrent-State Drift}
+
+\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Anonymous Authors}}
+
+\begin{document}
+\maketitle
+
+\begin{abstract}
+This paper asks whether native Open Compute Project (OCP) MXFP4 arithmetic can
+replace bfloat16 (BF16)-style arithmetic in a persistent-state Gated DeltaNet
+recurrence-core kernel on an AMD Alveo U55C field-programmable gate array
+(FPGA). Persistent state and the five-phase token schedule are inherited from
+prior work and held fixed. We implement E2M1 products in integer lookup-table (LUT) logic and
+E8M0-aware accumulation, then separate floating quantize/dequantize (Q/DQ)
+diagnostics from the native encoded path. At token \CorrectedLongTraceTokens,
+BF16 gives \CorrectedBfSixteenCosineEightK{} output cosine and
+\CorrectedBfSixteenStateRelLTwoEightK{} recurrent-state relative $L_2$ error
+against single-precision floating point (FP32). MXFP4 Q/DQ gives
+\CorrectedQdqMxfpFourCosineEightK{} and
+\CorrectedQdqMxfpFourStateRelLTwoEightK{}, whereas the native encoded path gives
+\CorrectedNativeEncodedCosineEightK{} and
+\CorrectedNativeEncodedStateRelLTwoEightK{}. The encoded HLS kernel uses
+\CorrectedUniformToBfSixteenLutRatio{} times the LUTs and
+\CorrectedUniformToBfSixteenStepRatio{} times the maximum estimated STEP cycles
+of the fixed-geometry BF16 kernel. A recurrence-aware residual/write-log
+candidate passes \CorrectedHeldoutSeedBlocks{} test seed blocks crossed with
+two initial-state conditions and two \CorrectedLongTraceTokens{}-token
+development conditions, but uses \CorrectedCandidateToBfSixteenLutRatio{} times
+the BF16 LUTs and misses \CorrectedCandidateFailedIiLoops{} explicit II=1
+constraints. Thus the present native MXFP4 implementations do not support
+replacing BF16. These are layer-level synthetic and HLS estimates, not a formal
+stability certificate or evidence of physical fit, closed-loop model quality,
+or board energy.
+\end{abstract}
+
+\begin{IEEEkeywords}
+Gated DeltaNet, MXFP4, recurrent state, FPGA, numerical drift, Vitis HLS
+\end{IEEEkeywords}
+
+\section{Introduction}
+Gated DeltaNet (GDN) replaces a growing attention history with a fixed-size
+recurrent matrix that is decayed and updated once per token
+\cite{gateddeltanet}. This makes the numerical state persistent: an arithmetic
+error written at one token can affect every later token. Lowering state
+precision therefore changes more than storage cost. It changes the dynamical
+system implemented by the decoder.
+
+Gupta et al. map GDN decode to a field-programmable gate array (FPGA) using persistent on-chip state and a
+five-phase BF16 pipeline \cite{gupta2026}. We use that organization as the
+baseline dataflow. Neither persistent state nor the five-phase schedule is a
+contribution of this work. We study only the arithmetic replacement inside that
+recurrence-core boundary.
+
+The Open Compute Project (OCP) MXFP4 format stores \CorrectedBlockSize{} E2M1
+elements with one E8M0 scale
+\cite{ocpmx}. Its \CorrectedMxfpFourLogicalBitsPerValue{} logical bits per value
+are attractive for a recurrent
+state that is read and rewritten every token. The shared exponent also creates
+two risks. Values below the block range disappear during alignment, and a
+scale change requantizes the complete block. Those errors feed back through
+the next state update.
+
+We evaluate the replacement as a controlled experiment. The recurrence,
+logical K-by-V state, layer dimensions, U55C target,
+\CorrectedTargetClockNs{} ns constraint, $P_K=\CorrectedPk$,
+$P_V=\CorrectedPv$, and block size \CorrectedBlockSize{} are fixed. The study
+contributes:
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item an encoded-integer MXFP4 recurrence oracle and native high-level
+        synthesis (HLS) arithmetic with explicit E2M1 decoding, E8M0 alignment,
+        round-to-nearest-even (RNE), Q1.15 controls, and event counters;
+  \item long synthetic traces at \CorrectedCheckpointSet{} tokens for
+        FP32, BF16, MXFP4 floating Q/DQ, native encoded MXFP4, flat INT4, and
+        a floating-Q/DQ MXFP8-state fallback, plus
+        \CorrectedScalePolicyCount{} scale
+        refresh policies;
+  \item a fixed-geometry BF16 versus encoded-MXFP4 HLS comparison and a
+        separately classified recurrence-aware correction; and
+  \item an evidence-backed negative answer for the current implementations,
+        including the numerical failure of native encoded MXFP4 and the hardware cost
+        of the correction that meets the frozen long-horizon synthetic gate.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+The evaluation covers one recurrence core and synthetic inputs. It does not
+measure Qwen3-Next perplexity, downstream accuracy, placed-and-routed cost, or
+board energy. Within that boundary, can native MXFP4 replace BF16-style
+arithmetic while reducing FPGA logic/schedule cost and limiting empirical
+recurrent-state drift over long synthetic decode traces?
+
+\section{Background and Scope}
+\subsection{Microscaling Representation}
+An E2M1 element contains \CorrectedETwoMOneSignBits{} sign bit,
+\CorrectedETwoMOneExponentBits{} exponent bits, and
+\CorrectedETwoMOneMantissaBits{} mantissa bit. Its positive finite magnitude
+codebook is $\{\CorrectedETwoMOneMagnitudeCodebook\}$. Both signed-zero codes
+decode to zero, and the encoder emits positive zero. An E8M0 byte encodes the
+shared power-of-two scale $2^{e-\CorrectedEEightMZeroBias{}}$ for codes
+\CorrectedEEightMZeroValidMinimum{} through
+\CorrectedEEightMZeroValidMaximum{}. Code
+\CorrectedEEightMZeroInvalidCode{} is invalid; an all-zero block uses scale code
+\CorrectedEEightMZeroZeroBlockScale{}. NaN and infinity inputs are rejected.
+These rules follow the OCP representation \cite{ocpmx}; scale selection and RNE
+at each state-write boundary are implementation choices.
+
+\subsection{Recurrence-Core Boundary}
+The head counts and K-by-V dimensions follow the pinned official
+Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B-Instruct configuration and Transformers implementation
+\cite{qwen3nextmodel,transformersqwen}.
+For value head $h$, query/key head $p(h)=\lfloor h/2\rfloor$ serves the
+corresponding pair of value heads. With logical state
+$S_h\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times V}$ and
+$K=\CorrectedKeyDim$, $V=\CorrectedValueDim$, the
+token recurrence is
+\begin{align}
+S_h^{d} &= \alpha_h S_h, \\
+u_h &= \beta_h\left(v_h-k_{p(h)}^\mathsf{T}S_h^{d}\right), \\
+S_h' &= S_h^{d}+k_{p(h)}u_h^\mathsf{T}, \\
+o_h &= q_{p(h)}^\mathsf{T}S_h'.
+\end{align}
+The query is normalized and scaled by $1/\sqrt{\CorrectedKeyDim}$ before entering the
+kernel; the key is normalized. Gated RMSNorm, the $z$ branch, projections,
+depthwise convolution, and output projection are outside the measured
+boundary. We therefore call the implementation a GDN recurrence-core kernel,
+not a complete GDN layer.
+Holding the unchanged convolution, projections, and output normalization outside
+the boundary isolates arithmetic-induced drift and cost in the persistent
+recurrence rather than mixing them with unmodified layer components.
+
+The operation order is decay, prediction from the decayed state, delta
+formation, rank-one write, and output from the updated state. The software
+oracle was checked against pinned Transformers and FLA recurrent, chunk, and
+cache paths before quantized experiments were admitted
+\cite{transformersqwen,fla021}.
+
+\subsection{Prior Work and Component Ownership}
+DeltaNet establishes the rank-one erase/write recurrence and parallel training
+formulations on which GDN builds \cite{deltanet}; the gated recurrence itself is
+inherited from Yang et al. \cite{gateddeltanet}. The persistent-state FPGA
+organization, paired-head handling, and five phases are inherited from Gupta
+et al. \cite{gupta2026}. MXFP4-B32 is standardized \cite{ocpmx}. QLlama maps
+microscaling quantization to FPGA inference, while Jack of All Scales studies
+MXFP dot-product mappings and digital signal-processing (DSP) block support on
+Agilex-5 \cite{qllama,jackscales}. MXFormer implements block-exponent-aligned
+MXFP4 compute in charge-trap memory, MicroMix mixes MXFP4/6/8 channels, and
+MXAttention and an MXFP post-training-quantization benchmark identify scaling,
+clipping, and underflow as material quality factors
+\cite{mxformer,micromix,mxattention,mxfpbenchmark}. These are precedents for
+alignment, mixed precision, and scale-policy studies. Zhang et al. quantize the chunk-wise GDN
+matrix-inversion path rather than token-serial recurrent state
+\cite{quantgdn}. KIVI retains recent full-precision entries beside a quantized
+cache, and SSDi8 uses a persistent INT8 state-space path with error correction
+\cite{kivi,ssdi8}. These works establish prior art for the component ideas. Our
+scope is the matched arithmetic-replacement experiment and its verification,
+not the invention of those constituents.
+Table~\ref{tab:ownership} makes the resulting ownership boundaries explicit.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\centering
+\caption{Component ownership relative to the corrected implementation.}
+\label{tab:ownership}
+\footnotesize
+\begin{tabular}{p{0.42\linewidth}p{0.22\linewidth}p{0.28\linewidth}}
+\toprule
+Component & Classification & Basis \\
+\midrule
