+
+Logically, B32 blocks run along the V-column axis within each K row. The code
+partitions the K and V access lanes by $P_K$ and $P_V$ and targets an initiation
+interval (II) of \CorrectedTargetIi{} on inner loops. Source directives request
+block RAM and UltraRAM bindings, but the inferred memory counts contradict
+bit-capacity lower bounds for the declared deep arrays and are excluded from
+paper conclusions.
+After removing invalid pipeline directives from long validation and
+requantization loops, HLS estimates a \CorrectedCandidatePathNs{} ns path. The
+\CorrectedTargetClockNs{} ns target and tool-reported uncertainty imply a
+\CorrectedConfiguredTimingBudgetNs{} ns effective budget, so the estimate
+misses the configured margin by \CorrectedConfiguredTimingShortfallNs{} ns.
+\CorrectedCandidateFailedIiLoops{} remaining targeted loops achieve
+II=\CorrectedFailedLoopIi{} rather than II=\CorrectedTargetIi{}. HLS reports
+\CorrectedCandidateDsp{} DSPs for coefficient and
+wider arithmetic; the E2M1 element multiplier itself remains integer logic.
+These source bindings and estimates do not establish a physically allocated
+\CorrectedNumLayers{}-layer bank.
+
+\section{Evaluation}
+\subsection{Questions and Controls}
+The experiments answer three questions:
+
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item Does native encoded MXFP4 limit empirical output and recurrent-state
+        drift over
+        \CorrectedLongTraceTokens{} synthetic tokens relative to FP32 and BF16?
+  \item Do floating-Q/DQ scale refresh policies or an MXFP8-E4M3 state
+        fallback meet the same finite-trace criteria?
+  \item If a recurrence-aware correction meets the finite-trace thresholds,
+        does its HLS estimate improve on the fixed-geometry BF16 kernel?
+\end{itemize}
+
+The controlled nominal baseline variants use \CorrectedNumQkHeads{} query/key heads,
+\CorrectedNumValueHeads{} value heads, $K=\CorrectedKeyDim$,
+$V=\CorrectedValueDim$, the
+same K-by-V state, and the same generated input prefix. The development seed is
+\texttt{\CorrectedSeedHex}. Required checkpoints are \CorrectedCheckpointSet.
+The nominal trace draws query and key vectors from independent standard normal
+distributions before normalization. Values and random initial states are
+zero-mean normal with standard deviations \CorrectedSyntheticValueStdDev{} and
+\CorrectedSyntheticInitialStateStdDev{}, respectively. Nominal decay and delta
+gates follow $\mathcal{U}[\CorrectedNominalAlphaMinimum,
+\CorrectedNominalAlphaMaximum]$ and
+$\mathcal{U}[\CorrectedNominalBetaMinimum,\CorrectedNominalBetaMaximum]$.
+The high-retention correction traces keep the vector distributions but use
+$\alpha\sim\mathcal{U}[\CorrectedHighRetentionAlphaMinimum,
+\CorrectedHighRetentionAlphaMaximum]$ and
+$\beta\sim\mathcal{U}[\CorrectedHighRetentionBetaMinimum,
+\CorrectedHighRetentionBetaMaximum]$, with both random and zero initial states.
+Changing the trace-family tag changes the deterministic RNG stream, even at the
+same seed. The correction results are therefore not paired numerical ablations
+of the nominal baseline trace.
+The frozen engineering gate requires output cosine at least
+\CorrectedOutputCosineThreshold{} at every checkpoint, final state relative
+$L_2$ at most \CorrectedFinalStateRelLTwoThreshold{}, and zero element
+saturation, accumulator saturation, and scale-clamp events. These are the three
+hard-event counters; alignment underflows and deliberate E2M0 residual clips
+are reported but excluded from the zero-event condition. We fixed these
+complementary diagnostics before test-set access: cosine constrains observable
+output direction, while state relative $L_2$ constrains hidden-state magnitude.
+They were not calibrated to downstream model quality.
+
+The correction uses \CorrectedHeldoutSeedBlocks{} test seed blocks crossed with
+random and zero initial states, producing \CorrectedHeldoutPairedConditions{}
+paired \CorrectedHeldoutTraceTokens{}-token conditions. Every artifact was
+deterministically recomputed by the same implementation and compared field by
+field with its recorded comma-separated values (CSV) artifact. This checks
+integrity and determinism, not implementation independence. The
+\CorrectedExtendedRunCount{} \CorrectedLongTraceTokens{}-token
+development runs use the same
+recorded seed with random and zero initial states. Each longer run was fully
+recomputed from source and compared field by field with its recorded CSV
+artifact. The encoded HLS candidate was
+also checked for \CorrectedArithmeticCsimCases{} frozen arithmetic cases and for
+\CorrectedStatefulCsimTokens{} stateful C-simulation tokens through repeated
+folds and final snapshot readback.
+
+\subsection{Metrics and Evidence Levels}
+Output cosine similarity flattens all value heads and V coordinates at each
+token. State relative $L_2$ flattens all heads, K rows, and V columns and is
+$\|S_q-S_{32}\|_F/\max(\|S_{32}\|_F,
+\CorrectedRelativeLTwoDenominatorFloor)$; maximum absolute state error and
+arithmetic events are also recorded. Exact equality returns cosine one and zero
+error; the artifact specifies the near-zero-norm cosine convention. No headwise
+minimum, percentile, or acceptance gate is computed. Software traces, HLS C simulation, HLS
+synthesis,
+register-transfer-level (RTL) simulation, post-route implementation, and board
+measurement are separate evidence levels. A PASS at one level does not fill a
+missing later level.
+
+\section{Results}
+\subsection{Uniform Arithmetic Replacement}
+Figure~\ref{fig:long} and Table~\ref{tab:long} show the controlled synthetic
+trace. BF16 remains close to FP32 through the final checkpoint. Both MXFP4 Q/DQ
+and native encoded MXFP4 fail the frozen criteria, and the encoded path drifts
+more than its floating diagnostic. Flat INT4 also fails. The floating-Q/DQ
+MXFP8-E4M3 state fallback
+improves on four-bit state but still misses both final criteria. Thus the Q/DQ
+curve cannot be paired with the encoded HLS cost as if they were one
+implementation.
+
