+\section{Discussion}
+The controlled answer is no for the current implementations. Floating Q/DQ
+already predicts substantial four-bit drift, while the native encoded path is
+worse and records \CorrectedNativeEncodedAlignmentUnderflowsEightK{} alignment
+underflows through token \CorrectedLongTraceTokens. The encoded HLS point also
+costs more than BF16. Adding a dense residual and recent-write log restores the
+finite-trace engineering criteria, but its worst longer-run state error is close
+to the boundary and its computation costs more than BF16.
+
+This result separates representation density from implementation cost. Shared
+scales reduce bits, but exponent comparison, alignment, requantization, and
+folding add control and arithmetic. The E2M1 multiplier is small and DSP-free;
+the surrounding recurrence is not. Table~\ref{tab:resource-ablation} localizes
+the dominant generated hierarchy. The correction's fold engine and update
+quantizer account for \CorrectedCorrectionMachineryLutPercent{} percent of its
+top-level LUT estimate. A useful replacement must reduce the full state-update
+path, not only the encoded element width.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\centering
+\caption{Generated HLS hierarchy ablation. Shares are relative to each
+variant's own top-level LUT estimate and are not routed utilization.}
+\label{tab:resource-ablation}
+\scriptsize
+\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\input{paper/corrected/tables/resource_ablation.tex}}
+\end{table}
+
+The word \emph{drift} is deliberate. For one head, let $E_t$ be candidate minus
+reference state. For the same external token inputs, define $R_t$ as the exact
+residual between the implemented candidate transition and the ideal reference
+transition applied to the candidate state. This residual includes input and
+state quantization, alignment, accumulation, scale, clipping, and fold error.
+The recurrence then gives
+\begin{equation}
+E_t=\alpha_t(I-\beta_t k_tk_t^{\mathsf T})E_{t-1}+R_t.
+\end{equation}
+For normalized $k_t$ and $\beta_t\in[0,1]$,
+\begin{equation}
+\lVert E_t\rVert_F\leq
+\alpha_t\lVert E_{t-1}\rVert_F+\lVert R_t\rVert_F.
+\end{equation}
+Let $P_t=k_tk_t^{\mathsf T}$. The parallel and orthogonal components obey
+the exact identity
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{split}
+\lVert(I-\beta_tP_t)E\rVert_F^2={}&
+(1-\beta_t)^2\lVert k_t^{\mathsf T}E\rVert_2^2\\
+&+\lVert(I-P_t)E\rVert_F^2.
+\end{split}
+\end{equation}
+Thus the delta gate attenuates only error parallel to the current key; the
+worst-case operator norm is one because $K>1$. Unrolling the one-step bound
+gives
+\begin{equation}
+\lVert E_T\rVert_F\leq
+\Bigl(\prod_{i=1}^{T}\alpha_i\Bigr)\lVert E_0\rVert_F+
+\sum_{j=1}^{T}\Bigl(\prod_{i=j+1}^{T}\alpha_i\Bigr)\lVert R_j\rVert_F.
+\end{equation}
+If $\alpha_i\leq\bar\alpha<1$ and $\lVert R_i\rVert_F\leq\bar R$, the
+right-hand side is at most
+$\bar\alpha^T\lVert E_0\rVert_F+
+(1-\bar\alpha^T)\bar R/(1-\bar\alpha)$.
+Our protocol permits decay arbitrarily close to one, so it supplies no uniform
+contraction margin. Changing keys may attenuate different directions, but a
+persistent-excitation bound is not established. These relations explain why
+repeated local errors matter, but they are not a stability certificate. We do
+not have a conservative bound on $R_t$ spanning
+input and base quantization, logged coefficients, folding, alignment,
+accumulation, scale changes, and clipping. ``Stable'' in the engineering gate
+therefore means only that the measured finite traces stay within its thresholds.
+
+The correction also changes the experimental role of MXFP8. Uniform MXFP8
+state is a stronger numerical baseline than MXFP4, while the corrected
+candidate's logical payload is only \CorrectedPercentBelowMxfpEight{} percent
+smaller. Without physical
+allocation and energy measurements, that difference does not justify the more
+complex representation.
+
+\section{Limitations and Threats to Validity}
+The numerical evidence is layer-level and synthetic. It does not include
+closed-loop Qwen3-Next execution, perplexity, downstream tasks, or confidence
+intervals over real text. The kernel boundary excludes convolution,
+projections, gated RMSNorm, model weights, activations, the full-attention
+layers, and their key-value cache.
+
+The nominal comparison uses one deterministic development seed. The correction
+uses a different high-retention trace family, which changes the RNG stream, so
+it is not paired with the nominal baseline. Its test set contains only
+\CorrectedHeldoutSeedBlocks{} independent token-stream seed blocks, each crossed
+with two paired initial states. The local candidate registration was not
+externally timestamped and omitted part of the execution dependency closure;
+its chronology and lack of leakage are therefore not independently auditable.
+Deterministic recomputation checks integrity, not a second implementation.
+
+Metrics flatten all heads. We report no per-head minimum or percentile, no
+confidence interval over a trace population, and no FP64 long-horizon curve.
+The cosine and state-error thresholds are engineering choices fixed before test
+set access, not thresholds calibrated to perplexity or downstream quality. The
+longer development result is only \CorrectedExtendedStateRelLTwoGateMargin{}
+below the state-error boundary. In a post-hoc sensitivity check, changing only
+that ceiling to \CorrectedStateRelLTwoSensitivityProbe{} would reject both the
+test-set aggregate and the longer development conditions. No formal contraction
+or accumulated-error bound is established.
+
+HLS synthesis estimates are not placed-and-routed measurements. The declared
+\CorrectedNumLayers{}-layer banks have not been physically allocated with
+padding, banking,
+replication, ports, scratch space, or SLR placement. Corrected-candidate RTL
+cosimulation, post-route timing, design-rule checks, bitstream execution, and
+Xilinx Runtime (XRT) parity are not run. Energy is not reported because no board
+telemetry was integrated; multiplying a power snapshot by simulated latency
+would not be a measurement.
+MXFP8 is evaluated only as a software state fallback; a matched MXFP8 HLS
+baseline is not available.
+No synchronized same-boundary GPU measurement is available, so we make no GPU
+latency or energy comparison.
+
+The HLS data planes are format-specific, and packing and token quantization are
+excluded. Command estimates address one layer at a time; no 36-layer service
+run, average service distribution, or p99 latency is measured. Verification is
+also asymmetric: BF16 has a four-command C-simulation check and no 64-token RTL
+trace, native encoded MXFP4 has 64-token C and RTL parity, and the correction
+has 64-token C parity but no 64-token RTL parity.
+
+The study uses one U55C target and one controlled parallelism point; cross-device
+portability and parallelism scaling are untested. Broader trace families and
+real activations remain necessary.
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+Native encoded MXFP4 does not replace BF16 in the evaluated persistent-state
+GDN recurrence core. Its empirical output/state drift is larger, and its
+fixed-geometry HLS implementation is larger and slower. A recurrence-aware
+residual and update log meets the bounded synthetic criteria, but its HLS cost,
+timing margin, and II results are worse than BF16. The paper therefore answers
+the replacement question negatively for the current implementations. Physical
+banking, routed timing, corrected-candidate RTL parity, closed-loop model
+quality, and measured board energy remain necessary before any stronger claim.
+
